{"authored" [{"question" "q-315550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(4t-7){e}^{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/state.json" "file_sha256" "c838dd4824375f10e4399d22f04118108b8b7c9bed1ddf4051b154cb3f3a8b91" "basis" 1213 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-7){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-7){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t} & >0 \\\\ 4t-3 & >0 \\\\ t & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/state.json" "file_sha256" "c838dd4824375f10e4399d22f04118108b8b7c9bed1ddf4051b154cb3f3a8b91" "basis" 1213 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "98c43c20ddedd7749da132c7bb1808e868642876f4fa196c15be2f8a9c0a56cf" "basis" 1213 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/state.json" "file_sha256" "c838dd4824375f10e4399d22f04118108b8b7c9bed1ddf4051b154cb3f3a8b91" "basis" 1213 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/state.json" "file_sha256" "c838dd4824375f10e4399d22f04118108b8b7c9bed1ddf4051b154cb3f3a8b91" "basis" 1213 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/state.json" "file_sha256" "c838dd4824375f10e4399d22f04118108b8b7c9bed1ddf4051b154cb3f3a8b91" "basis" 1213 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/state.json" "file_sha256" "c838dd4824375f10e4399d22f04118108b8b7c9bed1ddf4051b154cb3f3a8b91" "basis" 1213 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14031422/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-7109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{3}-6{t}^{2},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-7109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-12t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-12t) \\\\  & =6t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6t-12 \\\\ 6t & =12 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=2.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(2)=3(2)^{2}-12(2)=-12.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-7109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d16dfb324caef0b851b2245c5583e93cf6e6caa67570d8be777b5033c0dcf8ec"} {"question" "q-7109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-7109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=0,\\,v=0"] ["math" "t=1,\\,v=-5"] ["math" "t=2,\\,v=-12"] ["math" "t=2,\\,v=-6"] ["math" "t=3,\\,v=12"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-7109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=2,\\,v=-12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(4t-7){e}^{t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((4t-7){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{t}+(4t-7){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t} & >0 \\\\ 4t-3 & >0 \\\\ t & >\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d16dfb324caef0b851b2245c5583e93cf6e6caa67570d8be777b5033c0dcf8ec"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-315550" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2),$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the particle moves in the direction of increasing $x,$ then its velocity is positive. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\sqrt{t}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & >0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}} & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & >0 \\\\ t & >{e}^{0} \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (1,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335899" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d16dfb324caef0b851b2245c5583e93cf6e6caa67570d8be777b5033c0dcf8ec"} {"question" "q-335899" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335899" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335899" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335899" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by \n$s(t)=(4t-12)^{2},$\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in [${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,((4t-12)^{2}) \\\\  & =8(4t-12) \\\\  & =32(t-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 32(t-3) & <0 \\\\ t-3 & <0 \\\\ t & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$ is\n\n$$\nt\\in [0,\\,3).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335904" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d16dfb324caef0b851b2245c5583e93cf6e6caa67570d8be777b5033c0dcf8ec"} {"question" "q-335904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335904" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335904" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335904" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}ln\\,({t}^{2}),\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}ln\\,({t}^{2})=2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(2t)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+2t) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+6 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t={e}^{-3/2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,({e}^{-3/2}) & ={e}^{-3/2}(4ln\\,({e}^{-3/2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(4\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{-3/2}(-6+2) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t={e}^{-3/2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-4{e}^{-3/2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d16dfb324caef0b851b2245c5583e93cf6e6caa67570d8be777b5033c0dcf8ec"} {"question" "q-335917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^{-3/2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072876/state.json" "file_sha256" "7a42d97893b7322b9c85b4bd3affab65c28c8a4b374488c218b9a1ded3ca1a7f"} {"question" "q-335917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4e^{-3/2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/que